<section xml:id="sec-TITLE" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Polynomial functions and non-linear inequalites</title>

	<objectives>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				In this section, we learn how to:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<ol>
			<li><p>Find the zeroes or roots of a polynomial function, as well as their roots.</p></li>
			<li><p>Graph polynomial functions and analyze their behavior.</p></li>
			<li><p>Solve polynomial inequalities.</p></li>
    </ol>
	</objectives>

  <subsection>
    <title>Applications</title>

    <p>
      The skills we learn in this section will allow us to answer the following application questions:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The profit of a company is modeled by the function <m>P(x) = x^2+17x-42</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of units sold (in thousands) and <m>P(x)</m> is the profit (in thousands of dollars). 
            What is the minimum and maximum number of units sold that yields a profit (instead of a loss)?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The profit of a company is modeled by the function <m>P(x) = x^2+17x-42</m>, where <m>x</m> is the number of units sold (in thousands) and <m>P(x)</m> is the profit (in thousands of dollars). 
            What is the minimum and maximum number of units sold that creates a profit of at least <m>\$10,000</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A ball is thrown into the air. Its height (in feet) after <m>t</m> seconds is modeled by the function <m>h(t) = -16t^2 + 64t + 80</m>.
            State the first and the last moments in time when the ball is in the air.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

  </subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title>Roots or zeroes of polynomial functions</title>

    <p>
      Recall that a function is a rule that assigns to each input exactly one output.
      In the previous section, we have seen linear functions (whose graphs are lines) in the format <m>f(x) = mx + b</m> or using different letters <m>f(x) = ax + b</m>.
      We also saw quadratic functions (whose graphs are parabolas) in the format <m>f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> or the vertex format <m>f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let's take the linear function's general format of <m>f(x) = ax + b</m>
      and also take the quadratic function in the format <m>f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m>, 
      and ask: how could we extend this?
      We can examine <m>f(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d</m> which is called a <term>cubic</term> function.
      If we keep trying to go in this way (start at a new largest power of <m>x</m>, but include each power of <m>x</m> down to the constant term), we are going to run out of letters to use to represent the coefficients,
      so let's make the choice to have <m>a_2</m> be the coefficient of <m>x^2</m>, and <m>a_3</m> be the coefficient of <m>x^3</m>, and so on.
      If we continue the pattern, then <m>a_1</m> is the coefficient of <m>x</m>, and <m>a_0</m> is the constant term.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this way, we can write a function following this pattern where the highest degree of <m>x</m> is <m>4</m> as <m>f(x) = a_4x^4 + a_3x^3 + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0</m>.
      We can also write a function following this pattern where the highest degree of <m>x</m> is <m>5</m> as <m>f(x) = a_5x^5 + a_4x^4 + a_3x^3 + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0</m>.
      In general, we can write a function following this pattern where the highest degree of <m>x</m> is <m>n</m> as <m>f(x) = a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + a_{n-2}x^{n-2} + \cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0</m>.
      Note the three dots in the middle of the expression above, which is a shorthand way of saying "and so on" or "continue the pattern."
    </p>
    <p>
      Analogous to having two truly different forms of a quadratic function, we can also have two truly different forms of a polynomial function:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Every polynomial function can be written in the form <m>f(x) = a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + a_{n-2}x^{n-2} + \cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0</m>, where <m>n</m> is the <term>degree</term> of the polynomial function.
            This is called the <term>expanded</term> form of the polynomial function.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Every polynomial function can be written in the form <m>f(x) = k(x-r_1)(x-r_2)(x-r_3)\cdots(x-r_n)</m>, where <m>r_1, r_2, r_3, \ldots, r_n</m> are the <term>roots</term> or <term>zeroes</term> of the polynomial function.
            This is called the <term>factored</term> form of the polynomial function.
            If the same root appears multiple times, the factored form may also be presented more compactly using exponents. We will see some examples.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = 6x^5 + 4x^4 + 2x^3 + x^2 + 3x + 5</m> is a polynomial function of degree <m>5</m> in expanded form.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = 6x^5 - 4x^4 + x^2 + 3x - 5</m> is a polynomial function of degree <m>5</m> in expanded form.
            In this example, we can rewrite <m>f(x) = 6x^5 + (-4)x^4 + 0x^3 + 1x^2 + 3x + (-5)</m>,
            so we see <m>a_5=6</m> and <m>a_4=-4</m> and <m>a_3=0</m> and <m>a_2=1</m> and <m>a_1=3</m> and <m>a_0=-5</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>

    <p>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = 3(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-5)</m> is a polynomial function of degree <m>4</m> in factored form.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = 3(x-1)(x-2)(x-5)(x-5)</m> is a polynomial function of degree <m>4</m> in factored form.
            This function can also be written <m>f(x) = 3(x-1)(x-2)(x-5)^2</m> in factored form, which is a more compact way of writing the same function.
            Note that order of operations applies in reading this function, so the exponent <m>2</m> only applies to <m>(x-5)</m> and not to the entire product.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
      <example>
        <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x) = 3(x-1)(x+2)(x-5)(x-5)(x-5)</m> is a polynomial function of degree <m>5</m> in factored form.
            This function can also be written <m>f(x) = 3(x-1)(x-(-2))(x-5)^3</m> in factored form, which is a more compact way of writing the same function.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </p>

